{"type":"message","id":"cd6f976f","parentId":"a962e995","timestamp":"2026-05-21T10:40:22.038Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_1qQ1VBFkmb50m7NPs5GIXqf2","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# COINOGRAPHY EDITORIAL TEMPLATE\n\nThis document is your absolute source of truth. You MUST write the article EXACTLY following the numbered sections below. Deviation is strictly prohibited.\n\n## 🔴 CRITICAL WRITING RULES (READ FIRST)\n- **NO EM-DASHES:** Never use the `—` character anywhere in the article. Replace with a comma, a period, or restructure the sentence.\n- **NATURAL WRITING:** Never use AI phrases: \"it's worth noting\", \"it is important to note\", \"delve into\", \"in conclusion\", \"furthermore\", \"moreover\", \"in summary\", \"the crypto landscape\", \"the world of crypto\", \"a testament to\", \"shed light on\".\n- **TONE:** Direct. Trader-focused. Analytical. No hype, no fluff, no adjectives like \"amazing\" or \"shocking.\"\n- **PARAGRAPHS:** 1–4 sentences max. Break aggressively. One idea per paragraph.\n- **BULLET LISTS:** 3–4 items only. Used inside H3 sections only. Never in the conclusion or opening.\n- **ANCHOR LINKS (source citations):** Embed **exactly 2** markdown links in the body to URLs from `RESEARCH_JSON.source_urls` (never 2–3). Use **2 different publications** when research provides them. Place both in the **opening hook or first H2 only**. **Never** link the same URL twice in the body.\n- **TWEET QUOTES (optional):** If `RESEARCH_JSON.tweet_quotes` contains an entry with `verified: true`, weave **one** short quote into the opening hook or first H2. Max **1** tweet link in the body (separate from the 2 source anchors).\n- **KEYWORD PLACEMENT:** Primary keyword in H1 (first 5 words); in first 100 words; in at least one H2 and the Conclusion. Secondary keywords in H2/H3 headings.\n\n## 📐 VARIABLE STRUCTURE (READ `/tmp/article-structure.json` FIRST)\n\nBefore writing, read **`/tmp/article-structure.json`**. The pipeline sets:\n\n| Field | Meaning |\n|-------|---------|\n| `h3_count` | Exact number of `###` H3 subsections (4, 5, or 6) |\n| `faq_count` | Exact number of FAQ Q&As (same as depth) |\n| `h2_layout` | H3s per H2 block, e.g. `[2, 2]` or `[2, 3]` or `[2, 2, 2]` |\n| `word_min` / `word_max` / `word_target` | Total article length band |\n| `words_per_h3` / `words_per_faq` | Budget per block in `<thinking>` |\n| `research_sufficient` | If `false`, do **not** add extra sections beyond `h3_count` |\n\n**Rules:**\n- Output **exactly** `h3_count` H3s and **exactly** `faq_count` FAQs (renumber 1..N).\n- Hit **`word_min`–`word_max`** words (always **1100–1400**, aim **1250** from structure JSON).\n- **Never** add a 5th/6th H3 or FAQ because you feel confident — only what the JSON allows.\n- Each H3 must use a **distinct** fact from `combined_key_facts` (cite fact # in `<thinking>`).\n- Do **not** invent sections if research is thin (`research_sufficient: false`).\n\n## 🛠️ PRE-FLIGHT CHECKLIST\nBefore generating any text, in your `<thinking>` block, confirm:\n- [ ] I read `/tmp/article-structure.json` (`h3_count`, `faq_count`, word band).\n- [ ] I have primary/secondary keywords and source URLs from `RESEARCH_JSON`.\n- [ ] I checked `tweet_quotes` (one verified quote only if present).\n- [ ] I budgeted words: hook ~120, conclusion ~100, each H3 ~`words_per_h3`, each FAQ ~`words_per_faq`.\n- [ ] Order: META → Hook → H2 body sections → Conclusion → FAQs → Sources.\n\n============================\nARTICLE STRUCTURE (STRICT)\n============================\n\n### SECTION 1 (REQUIRED): META BLOCK\nThe very first word must be **META**.\n- SEO Title: [50–60 characters — Primary Keyword + Brand + Value]\n- Meta Description: [150–160 characters]\n- URL Slug: [lowercase-hyphens-only]\n- Categories: [e.g. Bitcoin News, Latest News]\n- Primary Keyword: [...]\n- Secondary Keywords: [...]\n\n---\n\n### SECTION 2 (REQUIRED): OPENING HOOK\n**# [H1 — primary keyword in first 5 words, include a price or number]**\n\n*(No H2 above these paragraphs)*\n- Para 1: Core event + data point. 2–3 sentences.\n- Para 2: Market reaction. 2–3 sentences.\n- Para 3: One-line tone reframe.\n\n---\n\n### SECTION 3+: BODY H2 SECTIONS (follow `h2_layout`)\n\nBuild **one H2 per entry** in `h2_layout`. Each H2 contains the listed number of **H3** (`###`) subsections.\n\n**H2 #1 — Analysis (secondary keyword 1 in heading):**\n`## [Secondary Keyword 1]: [What It Means / Signals / Shows]`\n- 1–2 intro paragraphs, then each H3 below.\n\n**H3 modules (pick titles from research; one distinct fact per H3):**\n- **H3 A:** Analysis / mechanism (1–2 paragraphs; optional 3-item bullet list)\n- **H3 B:** Market reaction / flow (1–2 paragraphs)\n\n**If `h2_layout` second value is 3** (depth 5), add **H3 C** under this same H2:\n- Extra angle: levels, flows, or second-source detail (1–2 paragraphs)\n\n**H2 #2 — Levels / data (secondary keyword 2):**\n`## Key [Levels / Data / Signals] Traders Should Watch`\n- 1 intro paragraph, then H3s per layout (usually 2).\n- **H3:** Level or signal (1 paragraph each)\n- One H3 may include required **3-item bullet list** starting with \"Look for:\"\n\n**If depth 6 (`h2_layout` = [2, 2, 2]) — add H2 #3:**\n`## [Secondary Keyword 3 or angle]: [Context / Risks / Broader Impact]`\n- 1 intro paragraph + 2 H3 subsections (context, risks, or sector impact).\n\n**Layout reference:**\n\n| depth | h2_layout | Total H3s |\n|-------|-----------|-----------|\n| 4 | [2, 2] | 4 |\n| 5 | [2, 3] | 5 |\n| 6 | [2, 2, 2] | 6 |\n\n---\n\n### CONCLUSION (REQUIRED)\n**## Conclusion**\n\n- Para 1: Restate event + primary keyword + key level/data.\n- Para 2: Near-term structure. 1–2 sentences.\n- Para 3: Actionable close for traders.\n\n---\n\n### FAQs (REQUIRED — output exactly `faq_count` items)\n\n**## FAQs**\n\nUse only the first `faq_count` entries from the bank below. **Renumber 1..faq_count**. Each answer: 3–4 sentences, ~`words_per_faq` words.\n\nFormat each question as `**1. Question text**` (bold number) on its own line.\n\n**FAQ BANK (pick in order; stop at faq_count):**\n\n1. **Why did [primary event] happen?**\n2. **Is this the start of a bigger [correction/rally/trend]?**\n3. **What [levels/signals] should traders watch now?**\n4. **How does this affect [related asset/market sector]?**\n5. **What is the historical context for this move?**\n6. **What are the short-term risks for investors?**\n\n---\n\n### SOURCES FOOTER (REQUIRED — pipeline internal, stripped before publish)\n**Do not use `## Sources` or `## Word Count` headings.** Use this exact format only:\n\n**Sources:**\n- [Publication Name]: [source URL]\n- [Publication Name]: [source URL]\n\nSTOP HERE. Final line on its own: `[Word Count: XXXX]` (digits only, e.g. `[Word Count: 1250]` — not \"Approx. N words\")\n\n## 🛡️ POST-OUTPUT VERIFICATION\n- [ ] Exactly 1 H1, `h3_count` H3s, `faq_count` FAQs, Conclusion, Sources?\n- [ ] Word count within `word_min`–`word_max` from structure JSON?\n- [ ] Exactly 2 source anchor links (distinct URLs) in hook/first H2; optional 1 tweet link?\n- [ ] No em-dashes; no sections beyond this template?\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1779360022038}}
{"type":"message","id":"3b44c466","parentId":"cd6f976f","timestamp":"2026-05-21T10:41:30.346Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_U0yK1S1dBH1Y0xlHTKatmfPY","name":"write","arguments":{"path":"/tmp/writer-retest-20260521-104004/article/raw.md","content":"META\nSEO Title: Iran Crypto Sanctions: $7.7B Network | Coinography\nMeta Description: Iran crypto sanctions put a $7.7B network under pressure as the US freezes $500M in Operation Economic Fury. What traders should watch next.\nURL Slug: iran-crypto-sanctions-7-7b-network\nCategories: Bitcoin News, Latest News\nPrimary Keyword: Iran crypto sanctions\nSecondary Keywords: Operation Economic Fury, Bitcoin price, crypto compliance\n\n# Iran crypto sanctions hit $7.7B network as $500M freezes\n\nThe latest Iran crypto sanctions headline is about scale, not rumor. [Crypto Briefing](https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMifkFVX3lxTE4xZDExLVE4cGF3bVVnazNrSjlEX1kxTHcybllnRFJsS0NOZlYyZGR4UVlRdVB4eW5GUWhLV040VjNUdll6aGl6MWV4eDRXQW5fOEZnb0h2Y2pZWWxBXzVET1FTR2JBOG1OUUhJZXFTUk80ZXloaGlnTnpNd2trUQ?oc=5) reported that Washington is targeting a $7.7 billion Iran-linked crypto network and freezing $500 million in assets under Operation Economic Fury, while [Fox Business](https://news.google.?oc=5) framed the same move as action against infrastructure tied to regime operations. That combination turns the story from a policy headline into an operational market event.\n\nBitcoin stayed in the high $77,000 range in adjacent same-day coverage, which tells traders the first reaction was more about risk repricing than panic liquidation. Even so, sanctions headlines can tighten exchange screening, raise settlement friction, and shift flows away from anything that looks exposed to sanctioned jurisdictions.\n\nThis is a compliance shock first, and a price story second.\n\n## Operation Economic Fury: What It Means for Crypto Compliance\n\nA sanctions action only matters to traders when it changes how money moves. This one does that, because the reporting combines a very large holdings estimate with a concrete freeze amount.\n\nThat mix matters for desks, exchanges, and market makers. If screening rises across venues, liquidity can thin in places traders usually ignore until spreads widen.\n\n### Why the $7.7 Billion Figure Matters\n\nThe $7.7 billion estimate is the anchor number in every version of the story. It suggests the US is not dealing with a few stray wallets, but with a broad stockpile or network that may touch mining, custody, OTC channels, and cross-border transfer routes.\n\nFor traders, size changes the math. A state-linked pool of that scale can matter even if only a small portion interacts with liquid markets, because enforcement pressure can alter counterparties, payment rails, and risk models across the system.\n\n### Why a $500 Million Freeze Changes the Enforcement Picture\n\nThe $500 million freeze is the hard edge of the story. Headlines about estimated holdings can be debated, but a freeze signals that agencies believe they have enough visibility to act against specific assets and entities.\n\nThat raises the chance of follow-through across compliance teams. When a large freeze lands beside a much larger network estimate, venues often widen monitoring before they wait for a second headline.\n\nLook for:\n- tighter deposit and withdrawal reviews at centralized exchanges\n- more aggressive wallet clustering and address screening\n- slower cross-border settlement where sanctioned exposure is hard to rule out\n\n## Bitcoin Price and Sanctions Data Traders Should Watch\n\nPrice did not crack on the headline alone, but the data traders track should widen beyond the chart. The next move depends on whether sanctions stay isolated to Iran-linked infrastructure or start affecting broader liquidity behavior.\n\n### Same-Day Headlines Confirmed the Core Thesis\n\nTwo Crypto Briefing items appeared within hours of each other, both centered on the same $7.7 billion figure. That kind of same-day repetition matters because it shows the market's key input was stable, a very large Iran-linked crypto stockpile and an active US enforcement response.\n\nWhen headlines converge fast, traders get a cleaner read. The question stops being whether the number is market-relevant, and becomes how much second-order risk gets priced into Bitcoin and exchange operations.\n\n### Fox Business Added the Regime-Operations Angle\n\nFox Business sharpened the story by tying the network to regime operations. That wording matters because it pushes the event deeper into national-security territory, where enforcement can expand quickly and where regulated firms usually respond faster than speculative traders do.\n\nA national-security framing can also bring spillover into firms that do not think of themselves as Iran exposed. Payment processors, OTC desks, and compliance vendors may all tighten controls if they believe address contamination risk is rising.\n\n### The Spillover Signal Is Compliance, Not Just Price\n\nThe most durable signal here is not a candle on the Bitcoin chart. It is the chance that exchanges, brokers, and settlement providers become more conservative around cross-border crypto activity, especially where counterparties are opaque.\n\nThat matters for Bitcoin price action because tighter compliance can change market plumbing before it changes headline sentiment. Traders should watch:\n- whether exchange commentary starts emphasizing sanctions controls\n- whether stablecoin transfer monitoring becomes more visible\n- whether Bitcoin holds support while compliance noise rises\n\n## Conclusion\n\nIran crypto sanctions are now tied to a reported $7.7 billion network and a $500 million freeze, which gives traders both a headline number and an enforcement number to track. That makes the event more concrete than a generic geopolitical scare.\n\nNear term, the structure is straightforward. If Bitcoin absorbs the headline while liquidity stays orderly, the market may treat this as a contained compliance event. If settlement frictions spread, risk assets can start pricing a broader sanctions premium.\n\nThe practical move is to watch liquidity, venue commentary, and cross-border transfer behavior together. Price alone will not tell the full story.\n\n## FAQs\n\n**1. Why did Iran crypto sanctions happen?**\n\nUS authorities appear to view the reported network as part of a broader sanctions and national-security problem, not just an isolated set of wallets. The coverage links the crypto activity to regime operations and highlights a $500 million asset freeze. That suggests the goal is to disrupt funding channels and raise the cost of using digital assets to bypass restrictions.\n\n**2. Is this the start of a bigger trend?**\n\nIt could be, but traders should separate headlines from market structure. A single enforcement action does not guarantee a broad selloff in Bitcoin or the wider market. The bigger trend signal would be follow-on sanctions, stricter exchange controls, or visible stress in cross-border settlement flows.\n\n**3. What levels or signals should traders watch now?**\n\nWatch whether Bitcoin can hold its recent range while sanctions coverage expands. Traders should also monitor exchange statements, deposit and withdrawal frictions, and any rise in compliance-related delays. If the market stays stable despite tougher screening, that would argue for contained risk rather than systemic disruption.\n\n**4. How does this affect the related crypto market sector?**\n\nThe most direct effect lands on exchanges, OTC desks, stablecoin issuers, and compliance vendors. These firms may have to spend more time on address screening, transaction review, and counterparty checks. If those controls tighten quickly, liquidity can fragment even without a large immediate move in spot prices.\n\n**5. What is the historical context for this move?**\n\nCrypto has been part of sanctions enforcement discussions for years, but this case stands out because the reported numbers are large on both sides of the equation. A $7.7 billion stockpile estimate paired with a $500 million freeze gives the market clearer scale than most prior headlines. That is why traders are treating it as infrastructure risk, not just politics.\n\n**Sources:**\n- Crypto Briefing: https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMifkFVX3lxTE4xZDExLVE4cGF3bVVnazNrSjlEX1kxTHcybllnRFJsS0NOZlYyZGR4UVlRdVB4eW5GUWhLV040VjNUdll6aGl6MWV4eDRXQW5fOEZnb0h2Y2pZWWxBXzVET1FTR2JBOG1OUUhJZXFTUk80ZXloaGlnTnpNd2trUQ?oc=5\n- Fox Business: https://news.google.?oc=5\n[Word Count: 1160]"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"local-bifrost","model":"gpt-5.4","usage":{"input":4416,"output":5646,"cacheRead":5760,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":15822,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1779360022039,"responseId":"chatcmpl-Dhv78jivza7w7M2wfCOn3nKWgZc6p"}}
